  "response_text": "The debugger allows the value actual value of the stack to be observed, rather than just the end value. The registers window shows this in more depth. In this window, registers can be seen representing specific memory, and using the debugger allows the user to see what is being stored in that memory location at a specified break point. This is beneficial to understand what exactly is happening behind the scenes to achieve the end goal. In this lab we are looking to see the in between steps rather than the end value. Printf would only just provide the end value. This can be compared to using the registers, where the inbetween steps of the memory is not seen from printf. For example, if the variable value got overwritten somewhere in the code, and by using printf this only prints the overwritten value, but by using debugger and registers, it allows the user to find exactly where their mistake occurred.\\n",
